","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":74,"newStart":1,"newLines":74,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_eventually_constant :\n    (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true) ∧\n      (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The centre column takes both colours infinitely often. This is the period-1 case of Prize 1, proved outright: an eventually constant sequence is eventually periodic, so this is the smallest piece of the prize that can be closed today, and nothing on the board closes any piece of it. Both halves are contradictions against Jen's theorem, and neither needs anything unproved.\n\nBLACK HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = true for every t ≥ N. `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t says centerColumn (t + 1) = !evolve t (-1); the left side is true, so evolve t (-1) = false, for every t ≥ N. Column -1 is then constantly white from N and the centre column constantly black from N, so both are eventually periodic with period 1 — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (That lemma's second column is `-1 + 1`, which needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHITE HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = false for every t ≥ N. `rule30_eq` at position 1 and time t reads evolve (t + 1) 1 = evolve t 0 XOR (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2), and evolve t 0 is false, so evolve (t + 1) 1 = (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2). Column 1 can therefore never turn from black to white after N. Two cases. If evolve t0 1 = true for some t0 ≥ N then evolve t 1 = true for every t ≥ t0 by induction, so columns 0 and 1 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent 0`. Otherwise evolve t 1 = false for every t ≥ N, and then the same identity forces (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2) = false, so evolve t 2 = false for every t ≥ N too — columns 0 and 2 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_pair 0 2`.\n\nWhy a proof of the wall would cite it: the mask law and `centerColumn_periodic_damage_white` in this tier both reason about the centre column's white cells, and this is what says those cells exist at all. It is also the base case of the only ladder in the prize's direction that anyone can currently climb.",","-    "disclaims": "This is the period-1 case of Prize 1 and nothing more. It says nothing about any period above 1, and no induction on the period is implied or available — the two halves use completely different arguments and neither generalises. It is not a step toward either P1 wall.",","-    "witness": {","-      "expression": "((List.range 12).any (fun t => centerColumn t)) && ((List.range 12).any (fun t => ! centerColumn t))",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "t < 12 — a finite shadow only. Both colours occurring among the first twelve cells is a necessary consequence of the statement, so this catches a flipped quantifier or a swapped true/false; it is NOT evidence for the infinitary claim, which no finite range can carry."","+    "id": "centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times (p N : ℕ)\n    (hc : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t)\n    (t : ℕ) (ht : N ≤ t) (hb : centerColumn t = true) :\n    evolve (t + p) (-1) = evolve t (-1) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE BLACK TIMES OF A PERIODIC CENTRE COLUMN PIN COLUMN -1, SO THE RESIDUAL OF P1 IS THE WHITE TIMES AND NOTHING ELSE. Why it is true: at a black centre cell the rule at the origin loses its middle input, so the next centre cell is the complement of the cell one to the left (centerColumn_succ_of_black). Read that at t and at t + p: a p-periodic centre column makes the two left-hand cells complements of the same bit, so they are equal. WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (open, size L, no route) becomes twelve lines with this in hand, and I have compiled that proof (explorer/seeder_scratch_p1d.lean, axioms propext and Quot.sound). The argument: suppose past some M every white time repeated column -1 too; this lemma covers the black times; so column -1 is p-periodic from max M N, column 0 is p-periodic from N, and not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1 forbids two adjacent eventually periodic columns. The same lemma is the black-time half of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times in this tier. Recommended for seeding by the theorist attack of 2026-09-08 ('the residual itself', C3: 'with the black times pinned by column_succ_of_black, the wall is a statement about column 1 at the white times of the centre column and about nothing else'); it was never seeded.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove P1 and does not weaken either wall. Its hypothesis is the negation of P1, so nobody can exhibit a p and N satisfying it and it carries NO WITNESS: a finite range would only confirm the hypothesis is unsatisfiable there, which is not what is claimed. It says nothing whatever about the white times, which is the entire open part. It is also not a settling or agreement-front statement and does not run into obstruction 69: it never reads a fixed bit of the packed row.",","+    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "have hbp : centerColumn (t + p) = true := by rw [hc t ht, hb]\n  have a := centerColumn_succ_of_black t hb\n  have b := centerColumn_succ_of_black (t + p) hbp\n  have e : centerColumn (t + p + 1) = centerColumn (t + 1) := by\n    rw [show t + p + 1 = t + 1 + p by ring]; exact hc (t + 1) (by omega)\n  rw [a, b] at e\n  exact Bool.not_inj e",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack"]","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",","-    "lean_name": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",","-    "statement": "theorem column_neg_one_damage_mask (X Y : Config) (t : ℕ)\n    (h0 : column X 0 t = column Y 0 t)\n    (h1 : column X 0 (t + 1) = column Y 0 (t + 1)) :\n    xor (column X (-1) t) (column Y (-1) t)\n      = ((! column X 0 t) && xor (column X 1 t) (column Y 1 t)) := by\n  sorry",","+    "id": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times\n    (h : ∀ p > 0, ∀ N : ℕ, (∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t) →\n        ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false → evolve (t + p) 1 = evolve t 1) :\n    ¬ IsEventuallyPeriodic centerColumn := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "A SUFFICIENT CONDITION FOR PRIZE 1, IN THE SHAPE THE WALL ASKS FOR: to prove that the centre column never repeats it is enough to control column 1 at the WHITE times of a hypothetically periodic centre column. Everything else is already pinned. Why it is true: split the times past the onset by the colour of the centre cell. At a black time, column -1 repeats for free (centerColumn_succ_of_black at t and t + p, the black-times lemma of this tier). At a white time, column_one_of_white says column 1 is the xor of the next centre cell with column -1, so the hypothesis plus the centre column's own period makes column -1 repeat there too. Column -1 is then eventually periodic and so is column 0, which not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1 forbids. WHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT: it is the model centerColumn_other_of_cohomologous_column in a second coordinate, and it converts the wall from a statement about an existential over all columns into a statement about ONE named column at a NAMED SUBSET of times. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1c.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound. By column_one_of_white the hypothesis is interchangeable with the same statement about column -1 at white times, which is exactly the negation of the open node centerColumn_periodic_damage_white; the two are one object seen from either side of the origin.",","+    "disclaims": "IT IS NOT THE WALL AND IT DOES NOT WEAKEN IT. What it buys is a smaller target, not an easier one: nothing here says the residual (column 1 at the white times) is any more tractable than P1, and I claim no evidence that it is. The hypothesis is a conditional whose antecedent is the negation of P1, so it is unsatisfiable in fact, no p and N can be exhibited, and the node carries NO WITNESS by design rather than by omission. Anyone who proves the hypothesis has proved Prize 1, which is the point and also the warning.",","     "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "disclaims": "NOT a sufficient condition for the wall. It is unconditional and true of every pair of pictures, so it cannot by itself rule anything out; it is machinery a proof by contradiction would use, not an implication into the prize. It also says nothing about the white times, which is where the open part lives — it only says that at a white cell the mask is open.",","-    "reason": "Two pictures that agree at the origin at times t and t + 1 differ at column -1 at time t exactly where they differ at column 1 AND the shared origin cell is white. A black cell at the origin erases the difference; a white one passes it straight through.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `sideways_inverse` at position 0, applied to the row `evolveFrom X t`, says column X (-1) t = column X 0 (t + 1) XOR (column X 0 t || column X 1 t), and the same for Y. XOR the two equations. The h1 hypothesis cancels the first term outright. What is left is (a || b) XOR (a || b'), where a is the shared origin cell (by h0) and b, b' are the two column-1 cells: that is false when a is black and b XOR b' when a is white, which is exactly the right-hand side. A `cases` on a and on the two column-1 Booleans closes it; the only real step is aiming `sideways_inverse` at the right row, since `column X i t` is `evolveFrom X t i` and the lemma is stated about a bare `Config`.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Read it with X the seed and Y the picture grown from row p (`evolveFrom_evolve` identifies that with the seed's picture read p rows later). Then the hypotheses are exactly 'the centre column repeats at lag p at times t and t + 1', which is what the wall's hypothesis supplies, and the conclusion says: the failure of column -1 to repeat at lag p is the failure of column 1 to repeat at lag p, masked off at every black cell of the centre column. Jen's theorem forbids column 1 from repeating and forbids column -1 from repeating, and this identity is the only bridge on the board between those two facts. Any proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour has to say what the mask does, and today nothing on the board says it.",","-    "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 9).all (fun t =>\n    !(decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 t) &&\n      decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + 1)\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + 1))) ||\n    decide (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) t)\n        (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) (-1) t)\n      = ((! column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t) &&\n          xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 1 t)\n            (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 1 t))))))",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "twelve deterministic 'noisy' rows v < 12, each against itself with position f in {1, 2} flipped, at every t < 9. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED: within this exact range the left-hand side is genuinely nonzero at (v, f, t) = (0, 2, 6), (5, 1, 8), (5, 2, 7) and (7, 2, 6), so the witness exercises the branch that carries the content. The seed itself will not do here — its white left cone means a difference planted at position d takes about 4d rows to creep back to column 1, and over initialConfig with d ≤ 4 and t < 12 both sides are identically zero (explorer/seeder_p1_witness2.lean). Independently: 0 failures in 76,711 hypothesis hits over 400 random configuration pairs, 2,241 of them with a nonzero left-hand side (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "rintro ⟨p, hp, N, hN⟩\n  have h1 := h p hp N hN\n  refine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1) ⟨⟨p, hp, N, ?_⟩, ⟨p, hp, N, ?_⟩⟩\n  · intro n hn\n    show evolve (n + p) (-1) = evolve n (-1)\n    have e : centerColumn (n + p + 1) = centerColumn (n + 1) := by\n      rw [show n + p + 1 = n + 1 + p by ring]; exact hN (n + 1) (by omega)\n    cases hc : centerColumn n with\n    | true =>\n        have hcp : centerColumn (n + p) = true := by rw [hN n hn, hc]\n        have a := centerColumn_succ_of_black n hc\n        have b := centerColumn_succ_of_black (n + p) hcp\n        rw [a, b] at e\n        exact (Bool.not_inj e)\n    | false =>\n        have hcp : centerColumn (n + p) = false := by rw [hN n hn, hc]\n        have a : evolve n 1 = xor (centerColumn (n + 1)) (evolve n (-1)) :=\n          column_one_of_white initialConfig n hc\n        have b : evolve (n + p) 1 = xor (centerColumn (n + p + 1)) (evolve (n + p) (-1)) :=\n          column_one_of_white initialConfig (n + p) hcp\n        rw [e, h1 n hn hc, a] at b\n        revert b\n        cases centerColumn (n + 1) <;> cases evolve n (-1) <;> cases evolve (n + p) (-1) <;> simp\n  · intro n hn\n    show evolve (n + p) ((-1 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((-1 : ℤ) + 1)\n    rw [show (-1 : ℤ) + 1 = 0 by ring]\n    exact hN n hn",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack", "Rule30.Proofs.ColumnOneOfWhite"]","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_neg_two_damage_derivative",","-    "lean_name": "column_neg_two_damage_derivative",","-    "statement": "theorem column_neg_two_damage_derivative (X Y : Config) (t : ℕ)\n    (h0 : column X 0 t = column Y 0 t)\n    (h1 : column X 0 (t + 1) = column Y 0 (t + 1)) :\n    xor (column X (-2) t) (column Y (-2) t)\n      = xor (xor (column X (-1) t) (column Y (-1) t))\n          (xor (column X (-1) (t + 1)) (column Y (-1) (t + 1))) := by\n  sorry",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "reason": "One column further left than the mask law, and there the mask disappears: the disagreement at column -2 is the plain discrete derivative of the disagreement at column -1. No masking, no `&&` — this is the one place in the leftward cascade where the relation is linear.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `sideways_inverse` at position -1 gives column X (-2) t = column X (-1) (t + 1) XOR (column X (-1) t || column X 0 t), and the same for Y. XOR the two. The bracket is (u || a) XOR (u' || a), where a is the shared origin cell by h0 and u, u' are the two column--1 cells: it is false when a is black and the column--1 disagreement when a is white. The two cases then join, and that is the whole trick: when a is black, `column_succ_of_black` applied to X and to Y (they share the origin cell at t by h0 and at t + 1 by h1) pins both column--1 cells to the complement of the same cell, so the column--1 disagreement is false there too. So in BOTH cases the bracket equals the column--1 disagreement at t, and the identity is the stated XOR of it with the one at t + 1.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Under a centre column repeating at lag p, it converts 'column -2 repeats at time t' into 'the column--1 mismatch is the same at t and t + 1'. So column -2 is eventually periodic exactly when the column--1 mismatch is eventually constant — and eventually constant means either eventually zero (column -1 repeats at lag p) or eventually one (column -1 repeats at lag 2p). Jen forbids both. That is a genuine structural fact about the assumed picture, in the same currency as the mask law and one column further in.",","-    "disclaims": "The cascade does NOT continue, and this is the honest limit of the statement rather than a gap in it. The same claim one column further left — that the disagreement at -3 is the discrete derivative of the disagreement at -2 — is measured FALSE: 418 failures in 35,795 hypothesis hits over random configuration pairs (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs). The reason is visible in the proof: at column -2 the second input to the `||` is still the shared origin column, and at -3 it is not. So a prover should not expect an induction here, and a seeder should not propose one.",","+    "id": "centerColumn_black_infinitely_often",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_black_infinitely_often",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_black_infinitely_often (N : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "HALF OF THE PERIOD-1 CASE OF PRIZE 1, AND IT IS PROVABLE: the centre column is never white for good. Why it is true: at a white centre cell the rule at position 1 loses its xor term and becomes an OR, so column 1 at the next step is column 1 or column 2 at this one. If the centre column were white from N on, column 1 could therefore never turn from black back to white after N, so it is monotone, so it is eventually constant, so it is eventually periodic — and so is the centre column, with period 1. Two adjacent columns cannot both be eventually periodic (not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = 0). WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (open, size L) is LITERALLY the conjunction of this node and centerColumn_white_infinitely_often, in that order — it closes as one anonymous constructor once both land. PROVED IN SCRATCH with a self-contained route needing no other node of this tier: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1e.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.",","+    "disclaims": "Nothing above period 1. There is no induction on the period here and none is available: this half turns on a monotonicity that exists only when the centre column is white at EVERY late time, and one black cell in a period destroys it. So it is not a step toward either P1 wall, and the tier does not pretend it is. A mathematician would call this easy and would very likely call it folklore; it is not in any source held here, but I did not search the literature and it should not be reported as novel. The witness is a genuine instance check rather than a shadow — every N below 8 really does get a black cell below 12 — but it says nothing about large N, where the whole claim lives.",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "by_contra hcon\n  push_neg at hcon\n  have hN : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false := by\n    intro t ht\n    have := hcon t ht\n    cases hx : centerColumn t with\n    | false => rfl\n    | true => exact absurd hx this\n  have mono : ∀ t, N ≤ t → evolve t 1 = true → evolve (t + 1) 1 = true := by\n    intro t ht hb\n    have e : evolve (t + 1) 1 = (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2) := by\n      rw [evolve_succ, rule30_eq]\n      norm_num\n      rw [show evolve t 0 = false from hN t ht]\n      simp\n    rw [e, hb]; rfl\n  have key : ∃ M, N ≤ M ∧ ∀ n ≥ M, evolve (n + 1) 1 = evolve n 1 := by\n    by_cases hex : ∃ t, N ≤ t ∧ evolve t 1 = true\n    · obtain ⟨t0, ht0, hb⟩ := hex\n      refine ⟨t0, ht0, ?_⟩\n      have all : ∀ s, evolve (t0 + s) 1 = true := by\n        intro s\n        induction s with\n        | zero => simpa using hb\n        | succ k ih =>\n            have := mono (t0 + k) (by omega) ih\n            rw [show t0 + (k + 1) = t0 + k + 1 by ring]\n            exact this\n      intro n hn\n      have e1 : evolve (n + 1) 1 = true := by\n        rw [show n + 1 = t0 + (n + 1 - t0) by omega]; exact all _\n      have e2 : evolve n 1 = true := by\n        rw [show n = t0 + (n - t0) by omega]; exact all _\n      rw [e1, e2]\n    · push_neg at hex\n      refine ⟨N, le_refl _, ?_⟩\n      intro n hn\n      have e1 : evolve (n + 1) 1 = false := Bool.not_eq_true _ |>.mp (hex (n + 1) (by omega))\n      have e2 : evolve n 1 = false := Bool.not_eq_true _ |>.mp (hex n (by omega))\n      rw [e1, e2]\n  obtain ⟨M, hNM, hcol1⟩ := key\n  refine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent 0 ⟨⟨1, one_pos, M, ?_⟩, ⟨1, one_pos, M, ?_⟩⟩\n  · intro n hn\n    show centerColumn (n + 1) = centerColumn n\n    rw [hN (n + 1) (by omega), hN n (by omega)]\n  · intro n hn\n    show evolve (n + 1) ((0 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((0 : ℤ) + 1)\n    rw [show (0 : ℤ) + 1 = 1 by ring]\n    exact hcol1 n hn",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent"]","+    },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 9).all (fun t =>\n    !(decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 t) &&\n      decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + 1)\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + 1))) ||\n    decide (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-2) t)\n        (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) (-2) t)\n      = xor (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) t)\n              (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n                (decide (i = f))) (-1) t))\n          (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) (t + 1))\n              (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n                (decide (i = f))) (-1) (t + 1)))))))",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "the same twelve noisy rows v < 12, flip f in {1, 2}, every t < 9. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). Non-vacuity: the column--1 disagreement is nonzero at four (v, f, t) inside this range, so the right-hand side is not identically false here. Independently: 0 failures in 76,711 hypothesis hits over 400 random configuration pairs, 4,398 of them with a nonzero left-hand side (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","+      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun N => (List.range 12).any (fun t => decide (N ≤ t) && centerColumn t))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "N < 8, witness t < 12"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",","-    "lean_name": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",","-    "statement": "theorem column_damage_zero_of_black_run (X Y : Config) (t j : ℕ)\n    (hagree : ∀ s ≤ j, column X 0 (t + s) = column Y 0 (t + s))\n    (hblack : ∀ s < j, column X 0 (t + s) = true)\n    (i : ℕ) (hi : i ≤ j) :\n    column X (-(i : ℤ)) t = column Y (-(i : ℤ)) t := by\n  sorry",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "reason": "A run of black cells in the shared centre column shields the left, one column per cell of the run. If two pictures have the same origin cell at times t .. t + j and that cell is black at t .. t + j - 1, then the two pictures agree at every position 0, -1, ..., -j at time t.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Write D i s for the disagreement between X and Y at position -i at time s, and prove by strong induction on i the triangle claim: D i s is false whenever t ≤ s and s + i ≤ t + j. At i = 0 that is hagree. At i = 1 it is `column_succ_of_black`, which at a black origin cell pins the cell at -1 to the complement of the origin cell one step later; both pictures have the same origin cell at s (hagree, since s ≤ t + j) and at s + 1 (hagree, since s + 1 ≤ t + j), and hblack applies because s - t < j. For the step, `sideways_inverse` at position -i gives column X (-(i+1)) s = column X (-i) (s + 1) XOR (column X (-i) s || column X (-(i-1)) s), and the same for Y; XOR-ing, D (i+1) s is false as soon as D i (s+1), D i s and D (i-1) s are, and the triangle's index arithmetic supplies all three. Reading the claim at s = t needs exactly i ≤ j.\n\nWHERE THE WORK IS. The induction is two-step — the case i + 1 needs both i and i - 1 — so it wants `Nat.strong_induction_on` or a paired invariant rather than plain `induction i`. The triangle bound is the other half: carrying `∀ s, t ≤ s → s + i ≤ t + j → ...` rather than a statement at the single time t is what makes the step go through.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. It is the quantitative form of the shielding the mask law shows at one column. Read with Y the picture grown from row p, it says: if the centre column repeats at lag p and has a run of j consecutive black cells at time t, then the j columns to the left of the origin all repeat at lag p at that time. So a proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour cannot come from the black cells — it has to come from what happens between the runs. That is where the difficulty actually lives, and the board has never said so in a theorem.",","-    "disclaims": "`i ≤ j` is exact, not an artefact of the proof: the next position along genuinely differs. Over random configuration pairs the two pictures differ at position -(j+1) in 17,596 of 311,892 hypothesis hits (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs), and 7 times inside the witness's own range (explorer/seeder_p1_live.lean). So there is no stronger version to reach for. This is a statement about black runs only and says nothing about the times between them, which is precisely the open part.",","+    "id": "centerColumn_white_infinitely_often",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_white_infinitely_often",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_white_infinitely_often (N : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE OTHER HALF OF THE PERIOD-1 CASE OF PRIZE 1: the centre column is never black for good. Why it is true: at a black centre cell the next centre cell is the complement of the cell one to the left (centerColumn_succ_of_black). If the centre column were black from N on, that reads as 'true equals not L', so column -1 is white at every time from N on — eventually periodic with period 1 — while the centre column is black at every time from N on, also eventually periodic. Adjacent columns cannot both be eventually periodic (not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1). WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: with centerColumn_black_infinitely_often it is exactly centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (open, size L), whose statement is the conjunction of the two in the other order. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1c.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.",","+    "disclaims": "Nothing above period 1, and no induction on the period is implied or available — this half and the black half use completely different mechanisms (one the left neighbour, one a monotonicity on the right), which is why neither generalises and why the pair is not a step toward either P1 wall. Almost certainly folklore in mathematical terms; not found in the sources held here, but not searched for either, so it should not be reported as novel. The witness is a real instance check for N < 8, not a shadow, and it is silent about every N above 7.",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "by_contra hcon\n  push_neg at hcon\n  have hN : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true := by\n    intro t ht\n    have := hcon t ht\n    cases hx : centerColumn t with\n    | true => rfl\n    | false => exact absurd hx this\n  refine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1) ⟨⟨1, one_pos, N, ?_⟩, ⟨1, one_pos, N, ?_⟩⟩\n  · intro n hn\n    show evolve (n + 1) (-1) = evolve n (-1)\n    have h1 : ∀ m, N ≤ m → evolve m (-1) = false := by\n      intro m hm\n      have hb := centerColumn_succ_of_black m (hN m hm)\n      rw [hN (m + 1) (by omega)] at hb\n      cases hx : evolve m (-1) with\n      | false => rfl\n      | true => rw [hx] at hb; simp at hb\n    rw [h1 (n + 1) (by omega), h1 n (by omega)]\n  · intro n hn\n    show evolve (n + 1) ((-1 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((-1 : ℤ) + 1)\n    rw [show (-1 : ℤ) + 1 = 0 by ring]\n    show centerColumn (n + 1) = centerColumn n\n    rw [hN (n + 1) (by omega), hN n hn]",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack"]","+    },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 4).all (fun j => (List.range 7).all (fun t =>\n    !((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s =>\n        decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s)\n          = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + s))) &&\n      (List.range j).all (fun s =>\n        column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s))) ||\n    (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i =>\n      decide (column (fun x => decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-(i : ℤ)) t\n        = column (fun x => xor (decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (x = f))) (-(i : ℤ)) t))))))",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "eight noisy rows v < 8, flip f in {1, 2}, run length j < 4, every t < 7. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED in this exact range: 167 hypothesis hits, of which 7 have the two pictures genuinely differing at position -(j+1) — so the range contains cases where the shield is exactly as wide as claimed and no wider, and the witness would catch an off-by-one in either direction. Independently: 0 failures in 311,892 hypothesis hits over 600 random configuration pairs, at j up to 12 (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs)."","+      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun N => (List.range 12).any (fun t => decide (N ≤ t) && !(centerColumn t)))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "N < 8, witness t < 12"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (p N : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p)\n    (hc : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t) (M : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ M, centerColumn t = false ∧ evolve (t + p) (-1) ≠ evolve t (-1) := by\n  sorry",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "reason": "If the centre column repeated with period p, then it would have infinitely many white cells at which its left neighbour fails to repeat with that same period. This is a consequence of the prize conjecture's negation — a step inside a proof by contradiction, in the same shape as the board's `centerColumn_other_of_cohomologous_column`, which also takes the periodicity as a hypothesis.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Suppose not: some M has no such t. Set K = max M N. For every t ≥ K the left neighbour repeats at lag p, for one of two reasons. At a WHITE cell of the centre column, that is the failed assumption. At a BLACK cell it is free: `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t gives evolve t (-1) = !centerColumn (t + 1), and hc turns centerColumn t = true into centerColumn (t + p) = true, so the same lemma at t + p gives evolve (t + p) (-1) = !centerColumn (t + p + 1), and hc again makes centerColumn (t + p + 1) = centerColumn (t + 1). The two are equal. So column -1 is p-periodic from K, and the centre column is p-periodic from N, so columns -1 and 0 are both eventually periodic — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (`-1 + 1` needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. A proof of either P1 wall assumes the centre column repeats and derives a contradiction. Ask what such a proof has to work with today and the honest answer is Jen's theorem and nothing else — Jen is a single negative fact, and no node turns it into anything a later step can use. This does: it converts Jen into a specific infinite set of times, the white cells at which the left neighbour breaks step, and those are exactly the times the mask law in this tier is about (at a black cell the mask is closed, so nothing can happen there). Together the two say where a contradiction must be found, which is more than the board says now.",","-    "disclaims": "The hypothesis is the negation of Prize 1, so nobody can exhibit a p and N satisfying it and the statement carries no witness — a finite range would only confirm that the hypothesis is unsatisfiable there, which is not what is claimed. It is NOT the wall and does not weaken it: it is one consequence a proof of the wall would derive, not a sufficient condition for it. Nothing here rules the hypothesis out; if it could, it would be the prize."","-  },","-  {","-    "id": "centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow (k m : ℕ) :\n    evolve (m * 2 ^ k + k) ((m * 2 ^ k : ℕ) : ℤ) = centerColumn k := by\n  sorry",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "reason": "The centre column reappears verbatim, arbitrarily far out on the right: the cell at position m * 2^k after m * 2^k + k steps is the centre column's k-th cell, for every m.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `rightDiagonal k j` is `evolve (j + k) j` by definition, so the statement is `rightDiagonal k (m * 2 ^ k) = rightDiagonal k 0` once `0 + k = k` is simplified. And `rightDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow` says `rightDiagonal k` is periodic with period 2^k from index 0 — exactly, with no onset and no transient — so `periodicFrom_mul` gives period m * 2^k from index 0, and reading that at n = 0 is the whole proof. The m = 0 case is the trivial one and is not vacuous cover: every m ≥ 1 is real content.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Nothing on the board relates the centre column to any cell away from the origin, and crystal 70 asks for exactly this as the opening move of the right-side route. The left-hand twin, `leftDiagonal_mul_pow_eq_settledCenter`, lands on `settledCenter`, a DIFFERENT sequence, because the left diagonals have transients and index 0 sits inside one; the right diagonals have none, so the same argument lands on the centre column itself. That difference is the whole of crystal 70's claim that the right side is a better position, and this makes it a theorem instead of a remark. Concretely it is a transfer principle: any statement about the cells (m * 2^k + k, m * 2^k), where the right-diagonal machinery is exact, is a statement about the centre column.",","-    "disclaims": "This is infrastructure, not an attack: by itself it proves nothing about either wall, and it is a short consequence of two closed nodes. It also does not touch obstruction 69's ratio — the centre column still outruns the left-hand settling front, and this says nothing about that. Its value is that it is the first node putting the centre column anywhere but the origin.",","+    "id": "column_one_succ_of_white",","+    "lean_name": "column_one_succ_of_white",","+    "statement": "theorem column_one_succ_of_white (X : Config) (t : ℕ) (h : column X 0 t = false) :\n    column X 1 (t + 1) = (column X 1 t || column X 2 t) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "COLUMN 1 CAN ONLY LOSE ITS BLACK AT A BLACK CENTRE CELL. Why it is true: the rule at position 1 is 'cell 0 xor (cell 1 or cell 2)', and a white cell 0 deletes the xor, leaving the OR. So at every white centre time column 1 moves upward and never downward. WHY THE TIER NEEDS IT: it is the mechanism of centerColumn_black_infinitely_often, and it is the forward-in-time companion of the two laws the board already has at the origin — column_succ_of_black (black centre, sideways) and column_one_of_white (white centre, sideways). Those two say what a white or black centre cell does to the column on its LEFT; this is the first that says what a white centre cell does to the column on its RIGHT, which is the column the residual of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times is about. Stated over an arbitrary configuration, like its two companions, so a half-line or damage argument can use it. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1.lean, axioms propext and Quot.sound.",","+    "disclaims": "Unconditional and true of every configuration, so by itself it rules nothing out — it is one unfolding of rule30_eq and a mathematician would call it a definition chase, not a result. It gives monotonicity only across a white centre cell; at a black one column 1 becomes the complement of the same OR and the monotonicity is gone, which is exactly why the period-1 argument it feeds does not reach period 2. WITNESS NON-VACUITY CHECKED: the hypothesis holds at t = 2, 6, 7 in range over the seed, and the same expression with column 3 in place of column 2 evaluates to false, so the check can fail.",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "unfold column at h ⊢\n  rw [evolveFrom_succ, rule30_eq]\n  norm_num\n  rw [h]\n  simp",","+      "imports": []","+    },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "([(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3), (1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1)] : List (ℕ × ℕ)).all (fun km => decide (evolve (km.2 * 2 ^ km.1 + km.1) ((km.2 * 2 ^ km.1 : ℕ) : ℤ) = centerColumn km.1))",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "the pairs (k, m) listed, so k ≤ 3 and m ≤ 3, the deepest being k = 3, m = 1 — cell 8 of row 11. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). The list is explicit rather than a range because the cost is 3^(m * 2^k + k): k = 2, m = 3 is row 15 and already 14 million neighbour evaluations, and k = 3, m = 2 is row 19 and out of reach. Independently checked to k ≤ 6, m ≤ 4 — 35 pairs, 0 failures — with the array engine (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","+      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun t => column initialConfig 0 t || (column initialConfig 1 (t + 1) == (column initialConfig 1 t || column initialConfig 2 t)))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "t < 12 over the seed, hypothesis-guarded"","     }","   }"," ]}"]}],"originalFile":"{"proposals": [
  {
    "id": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",
    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",
    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_eventually_constant :\n    (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true) ∧\n      (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false) := by\n  sorry",
    "reason": "The centre column takes both colours infinitely often. This is the period-1 case of Prize 1, proved outright: an eventually constant sequence is eventually periodic, so this is the smallest piece of the prize that can be closed today, and nothing on the board closes any piece of it. Both halves are contradictions against Jen's theorem, and neither needs anything unproved.\n\nBLACK HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = true for every t ≥ N. `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t says centerColumn (t + 1) = !evolve t (-1); the left side is true, so evolve t (-1) = false, for every t ≥ N. Column -1 is then constantly white from N and the centre column constantly black from N, so both are eventually periodic with period 1 — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (That lemma's second column is `-1 + 1`, which needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHITE HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = false for every t ≥ N. `rule30_eq` at position 1 and time t reads evolve (t + 1) 1 = evolve t 0 XOR (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2), and evolve t 0 is false, so evolve (t + 1) 1 = (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2). Column 1 can therefore never turn from black to white after N. Two cases. If evolve t0 1 = true for some t0 ≥ N then evolve t 1 = true for every t ≥ t0 by induction, so columns 0 and 1 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent 0`. Otherwise evolve t 1 = false for every t ≥ N, and then the same identity forces (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2) = false, so evolve t 2 = false for every t ≥ N too — columns 0 and 2 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_pair 0 2`.\n\nWhy a proof of the wall would cite it: the mask law and `centerColumn_periodic_damage_white` in this tier both reason about the centre column's white cells, and this is what says those cells exist at all. It is also the base case of the only ladder in the prize's direction that anyone can currently climb.",
    "disclaims": "This is the period-1 case of Prize 1 and nothing more. It says nothing about any period above 1, and no induction on the period is implied or available — the two halves use completely different arguments and neither generalises. It is not a step toward either P1 wall.",
