    "id": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",
    "lean_name": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",
    "statement": "theorem column_neg_one_damage_mask (X Y : Config) (t : ℕ)\n    (h0 : column X 0 t = column Y 0 t)\n    (h1 : column X 0 (t + 1) = column Y 0 (t + 1)) :\n    xor (column X (-1) t) (column Y (-1) t)\n      = ((! column X 0 t) && xor (column X 1 t) (column Y 1 t)) := by\n  sorry",
    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",
    "disclaims": "NOT a sufficient condition for the wall. It is unconditional and true of every pair of pictures, so it cannot by itself rule anything out; it is machinery a proof by contradiction would use, not an implication into the prize. It also says nothing about the white times, which is where the open part lives — it only says that at a white cell the mask is open.",
    "reason": "Two pictures that agree at the origin at times t and t + 1 differ at column -1 at time t exactly where they differ at column 1 AND the shared origin cell is white. A black cell at the origin erases the difference; a white one passes it straight through.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `sideways_inverse` at position 0, applied to the row `evolveFrom X t`, says column X (-1) t = column X 0 (t + 1) XOR (column X 0 t || column X 1 t), and the same for Y. XOR the two equations. The h1 hypothesis cancels the first term outright. What is left is (a || b) XOR (a || b'), where a is the shared origin cell (by h0) and b, b' are the two column-1 cells: that is false when a is black and b XOR b' when a is white, which is exactly the right-hand side. A `cases` on a and on the two column-1 Booleans closes it; the only real step is aiming `sideways_inverse` at the right row, since `column X i t` is `evolveFrom X t i` and the lemma is stated about a bare `Config`.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Read it with X the seed and Y the picture grown from row p (`evolveFrom_evolve` identifies that with the seed's picture read p rows later). Then the hypotheses are exactly 'the centre column repeats at lag p at times t and t + 1', which is what the wall's hypothesis supplies, and the conclusion says: the failure of column -1 to repeat at lag p is the failure of column 1 to repeat at lag p, masked off at every black cell of the centre column. Jen's theorem forbids column 1 from repeating and forbids column -1 from repeating, and this identity is the only bridge on the board between those two facts. Any proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour has to say what the mask does, and today nothing on the board says it.",
    "witness": {
      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 9).all (fun t =>\n    !(decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 t) &&\n      decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + 1)\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + 1))) ||\n    decide (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) t)\n        (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) (-1) t)\n      = ((! column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t) &&\n          xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 1 t)\n            (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 1 t))))))",
      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],
      "range": "twelve deterministic 'noisy' rows v < 12, each against itself with position f in {1, 2} flipped, at every t < 9. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED: within this exact range the left-hand side is genuinely nonzero at (v, f, t) = (0, 2, 6), (5, 1, 8), (5, 2, 7) and (7, 2, 6), so the witness exercises the branch that carries the content. The seed itself will not do here — its white left cone means a difference planted at position d takes about 4d rows to creep back to column 1, and over initialConfig with d ≤ 4 and t < 12 both sides are identically zero (explorer/seeder_p1_witness2.lean). Independently: 0 failures in 76,711 hypothesis hits over 400 random configuration pairs, 2,241 of them with a nonzero left-hand side (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."
    }
