    "id": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",
    "lean_name": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",
    "statement": "theorem column_damage_zero_of_black_run (X Y : Config) (t j : ℕ)\n    (hagree : ∀ s ≤ j, column X 0 (t + s) = column Y 0 (t + s))\n    (hblack : ∀ s < j, column X 0 (t + s) = true)\n    (i : ℕ) (hi : i ≤ j) :\n    column X (-(i : ℤ)) t = column Y (-(i : ℤ)) t := by\n  sorry",
    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",
    "reason": "A run of black cells in the shared centre column shields the left, one column per cell of the run. If two pictures have the same origin cell at times t .. t + j and that cell is black at t .. t + j - 1, then the two pictures agree at every position 0, -1, ..., -j at time t.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Write D i s for the disagreement between X and Y at position -i at time s, and prove by strong induction on i the triangle claim: D i s is false whenever t ≤ s and s + i ≤ t + j. At i = 0 that is hagree. At i = 1 it is `column_succ_of_black`, which at a black origin cell pins the cell at -1 to the complement of the origin cell one step later; both pictures have the same origin cell at s (hagree, since s ≤ t + j) and at s + 1 (hagree, since s + 1 ≤ t + j), and hblack applies because s - t < j. For the step, `sideways_inverse` at position -i gives column X (-(i+1)) s = column X (-i) (s + 1) XOR (column X (-i) s || column X (-(i-1)) s), and the same for Y; XOR-ing, D (i+1) s is false as soon as D i (s+1), D i s and D (i-1) s are, and the triangle's index arithmetic supplies all three. Reading the claim at s = t needs exactly i ≤ j.\n\nWHERE THE WORK IS. The induction is two-step — the case i + 1 needs both i and i - 1 — so it wants `Nat.strong_induction_on` or a paired invariant rather than plain `induction i`. The triangle bound is the other half: carrying `∀ s, t ≤ s → s + i ≤ t + j → ...` rather than a statement at the single time t is what makes the step go through.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. It is the quantitative form of the shielding the mask law shows at one column. Read with Y the picture grown from row p, it says: if the centre column repeats at lag p and has a run of j consecutive black cells at time t, then the j columns to the left of the origin all repeat at lag p at that time. So a proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour cannot come from the black cells — it has to come from what happens between the runs. That is where the difficulty actually lives, and the board has never said so in a theorem.",
    "disclaims": "`i ≤ j` is exact, not an artefact of the proof: the next position along genuinely differs. Over random configuration pairs the two pictures differ at position -(j+1) in 17,596 of 311,892 hypothesis hits (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs), and 7 times inside the witness's own range (explorer/seeder_p1_live.lean). So there is no stronger version to reach for. This is a statement about black runs only and says nothing about the times between them, which is precisely the open part.",
    "witness": {
      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 4).all (fun j => (List.range 7).all (fun t =>\n    !((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s =>\n        decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s)\n          = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + s))) &&\n      (List.range j).all (fun s =>\n        column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s))) ||\n    (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i =>\n      decide (column (fun x => decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-(i : ℤ)) t\n        = column (fun x => xor (decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (x = f))) (-(i : ℤ)) t))))))",
      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],
      "range": "eight noisy rows v < 8, flip f in {1, 2}, run length j < 4, every t < 7. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED in this exact range: 167 hypothesis hits, of which 7 have the two pictures genuinely differing at position -(j+1) — so the range contains cases where the shield is exactly as wide as claimed and no wider, and the witness would catch an off-by-one in either direction. Independently: 0 failures in 311,892 hypothesis hits over 600 random configuration pairs, at j up to 12 (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs)."
    }
